	<item>
	  <headword id="idola62">[Te Ruadh Mhic Mhuirich]</headword>
	  <description>Mac Mhuirich in Uist asked his red haired daughter to demand of him her dowry and Te Ruadh Mhic Mhuirich requested: “Seicein, Meicein m’ aghan meadhrach / Bo dhubh, bo dhonn, bo chrom riabhach / Sliochd na h-aona bà maoile ruadha / Nach do dh’fhàg a bhuaile riamh na h-aonar / Agus an tarbh mogach, donn.” – the lot.</description>
	</item>  
    <item><headword id="d0e233" type="heading" level="h1">4. Names for certain beasts, e.g. an animal on which the owner bestows particular attention</headword></item>
    <item>
	  <headword id="d0e238">[moladh]</headword>
	  <description>Did not want to praise their own cattle too much in case they might suffer loss of them: Air eagal ’s gu mìll moladh i.</description>
    </item>
	<item>
	  <headword id="d0e247">[dosgainn]</headword>
	  <description>If a beast died, a person on hearing it said: “Dosgainn na bliadhna gu robh leatha (leis).” (May the years loss be gone with it.)</description>
	</item>
	<item>
	  <headword id="d0e256">[buachailleachd]</headword>
	  <description> “Buachailleachd Chalum Chill’ oirre.” (May St Columba keep watch over her.)</description>
	</item>
